= What is measured

Solve time on pigeonhole instances PHP($n+1$, $n$): $n+1$ pigeons into $n$ holes,
which is UNSAT and forces a CDCL solver through (super-)exponential search. These
are ideal for a runtime comparison because the work is pure search — no I/O bias,
deterministic, and it scales sharply with $n$, so we can land an instance in the
few-seconds range where actual solving (not process startup) dominates.

Instances are generated by `shadow/tools/gen_cnf.py` (up to php_8_7) and
`shadow/tools/gen_big_php.py 8 9` (php_9_8, php_10_9).

/ C: original MiniSat reference, `clang++ -O2 -std=c++11`, whole-process wall time
  (parse + solve + exit; parse/IO is negligible vs solve on these instances).
/ Kotlin/JVM: `:minisat:runBenchmark`, `solve()` only, median of 5 runs after 3
  warm-ups (the JIT must warm up before numbers mean anything).
/ Kotlin/Native: the macOS arm64 *release* (`-opt`) executable
  (`:minisat:linkReleaseExecutableMacosArm64`), `solve()` only, same median-of-5
  after-3-warm-up protocol, timed with `TimeSource.Monotonic`.

Machine: Apple Silicon (arm64), macOS. All three run the SAME MiniSat algorithm
(the C reference and the shared Kotlin port in `:minisat`).

= Results

#table(
  columns: 6,
  align: (left, right, right, right, right, right),
  table.header[instance][C][Kotlin/JVM][Kotlin/Native][JVM / C][Native / C],
  [php_9_8],  [0.180 s], [0.239 s], [0.650 s], [1.33×], [3.61×],
  [php_10_9], [2.606 s], [3.526 s], [9.641 s], [1.35×], [3.70×],
)

The ratios are stable across both instances:

- *Kotlin/JVM ≈ 1.3× C* — a small constant factor; the port is competitive.
- *Kotlin/Native ≈ 3.7× C* — and, notably, *slower than Kotlin/JVM* (≈ 2.7× the JVM
  time).

= Why is Kotlin/Native slower than the JVM here?

This surprises people ("native should be faster"), but it is a known effect for this
kind of workload. "Native" means *no VM at runtime*, not *as fast as C*.

+ *JIT beats AOT on long-running hot loops.* The JVM is not interpreting here: HotSpot
  profiles the `solve()` hot loop at runtime and recompiles it with optimizations a
  static compiler cannot safely apply — profile-guided inlining along the paths
  actually taken, bounds-check elimination on the hot arrays, devirtualization of
  monomorphic calls, loop unrolling. For a solve that runs for seconds, the compile
  cost is fully amortized. Kotlin/Native is *ahead-of-time* (LLVM) and must stay
  conservative — it has no runtime profile.
+ *The workload is allocation- and array-heavy.* CDCL solvers hammer arrays,
  watchlists, and clause allocation. What matters is how well the compiler eliminates
  bounds checks, does escape analysis (stack- vs heap-allocation), and how the GC
  behaves. The C reference has *no bounds checks and no GC at all*; the JVM claws a lot
  of that back with a mature JIT + generational GC; Kotlin/Native (LLVM backend, newer
  GC) is currently less favourable for exactly this pattern.
+ *GC model.* JVM: a highly-tuned throughput GC. Kotlin/Native: a newer GC, less suited
  to this allocation storm. C: none.

So the ordering C < JVM < Native (slower) is plausible: C has zero runtime overhead,
the JVM recovers most of the gap via JIT + a mature GC, and Kotlin/Native pays an AOT
penalty *without* C's zero-overhead memory model.

#note(title: "Control: the release build really is optimized")[
  To rule out a measurement artifact, the *debug* native binary
  (`linkDebugExecutableMacosArm64`) solves php_9_8 in *7860 ms* — ~12× slower than the
  *release* binary's 650 ms. So the 650 ms figure is genuinely the `-opt` code, and the
  ~3.6× gap to C is real, not an un-optimized build.
]

#warn[
  These are *JVM and macOS-arm64-native* numbers on one machine. They are not a claim
  about every platform or every instance family — but the JVM/C and Native/C ratios are
  each stable across the two PHP sizes measured. No perf statement is made without these
  measured numbers (see also `shadow/BENCHMARK_RESULTS.md` for the raw table).

  For the game (luckySweeper) this matters: it runs the Kotlin MiniSat on the *JVM*
  (Android/desktop), i.e. the ~1.3×-of-C column — the fast one. Kotlin/Native would be
  the relevant column only for an iOS/native build of the solver.
]

= Reproduce

```
# C reference
clang++ -O2 -std=c++11 -o shadow/minisat-c/minisat_bench shadow/minisat-c/minisat_trace.cc
time shadow/minisat-c/minisat_bench shadow/cnf/php_10_9.cnf

# Kotlin/JVM
./gradlew :minisat:runBenchmark --args="$(pwd)/shadow/cnf/php_10_9.cnf"

# Kotlin/Native (macOS arm64, release)
./gradlew :minisat:linkReleaseExecutableMacosArm64
minisat/build/bin/macosArm64/releaseExecutable/minisat.kexe \
  shadow/cnf/php_9_8.cnf shadow/cnf/php_10_9.cnf
```
